# Testing Engineering

Verify behavior and risk controls according to `docs/TESTING_STRATEGY.md`.
Tests must be deterministic, independent, and diagnostic.

## Test Design

- Test public behavior, domain invariants, boundaries, and refusal paths—not private implementation details.
- Name tests `test_<behavior>_<condition>_<outcome>` and keep Arrange/Act/Assert visible.
- Add a regression test that fails before the fix for every defect.
- Use parametrization for meaningful cases and Hypothesis for invariants and edge spaces.
- Assert complete result semantics, including estimator, sample size, time window, units, and provenance.
- Never snapshot the current implementation as an expected statistical value.

## Test Tiers

- Unit tests isolate domain, analytics, and application behavior.
- Statistical tests cite independently obtainable NIST/SEMATECH or Montgomery expected values.
- Integration tests exercise real adapter boundaries; contract tests run the same repository contract against every adapter.
- End-to-end tests cover only critical user journeys. Performance tests enforce the PRD budgets.
- Mark every non-unit tier explicitly. Run `statistical/` at pre-commit and in its own CI stage; schedule the other non-unit tiers in CI.

## Doubles, Coverage, and CI

- Fake application ports at unit boundaries; mock external effects only. Do not mock domain entities or numerical functions.
- Patch where a dependency is used, never where it is defined.
- Fix time, random seeds, locale, and timezone. Tests must not depend on order, network access, or developer state.
- Treat coverage as missing-evidence detection: domain and analytics require near-exhaustive branch coverage, with no regression.
- Keep tests parallel-safe and give failures the inputs, expected result, actual result, and source citation needed to diagnose them.
